ns.ZoneLore = {
[1411]={articles={"Durotar"},text="Durotar, named after Thrall's father Durotan, lies on the eastern coast of Kalimdor, between the Barrens and Azshara. Its land is rocky, red and cracked, hot and dry, much like the orcs' lost homeland of Draenor, and dangerous creatures shelter in its crags and canyons.\n\nYoung orcs prove themselves in the Valley of Trials before facing the world beyond. Since the orcs founded Orgrimmar, Durotar has become a home for all the Horde's races, above all the Darkspear trolls of the Echo Isles.",chapters={{after=794,articles={"Vile Familiars (Classic)","Burning Blade Medallion (Classic)"},text="The trials of the Valley were meant to be the first test of a young warrior, but a taint has crept in. The Burning Blade, a cult that worships demons, has made a coven in a cave in the valley's hills, and its vile familiars spilled out to cause havoc. The medallion of power hidden in the coven is out of the cult's hands now, but Zureetha Fargaze fears the Burning Blade has greater aims in Durotar."},{after=784,articles={"Vanquish the Betrayers"},text="Years ago, Admiral Proudmoore led the humans of Kul Tiras onto Durotar's shores, breaking the pact Thrall had made with Jaina Proudmoore to defeat Archimonde. The Horde threw them back and Tiragarde Keep fell, but the Admiral's reserves under Lieutenant Benedict retook its ruins. Benedict is dead now, and many of his sailors and marines with him."},{after=831,articles={"The Admiral's Orders","The Admiral's Orders (2)"},text="A weathered envelope sealed with Admiral Proudmoore's crest held his orders. The Admiral is dead, but he laid his plans before he fell, and Gar'Thok of Razor Hill fears that more of his men will land on Durotar's shores. Vol'jin, who long urged Thrall not to trust the humans, has taken the orders to the Warchief himself."},{after=786,articles={"Thwarting Kolkar Aggression"},text="Over the ridge west of Razor Hill, the Kolkar centaurs of Kolkar Crag were planning a three-pronged attack on the orcs and trolls of Durotar, its plans divided among three of their leaders. Those plans are destroyed and the invasion stopped before it began, though Lar Prowltusk is sure there will be blood spilled before these troubled times are through."},{after=826,articles={"Report to Sen'jin Village (Classic)","Zalazane (quest)"},text="The Darkspear trolls are the orcs' honourable friends, but they have troubles of their own. The Echo Isles off the coast were once their home, until the witch doctor Zalazane took them; from there he sent his trolls to the mainland to hex the Darkspear and drag them under his sway. Zalazane is dead, his head brought to Master Gadrin, and Sen'jin Village can sleep in peace again."},{after=99048,articles={"A Missing Hand"},text="Orgnil Soulscar had a scout of the Shattered Hand, a band of subtler fighters, watching Tiragarde Keep from a distance, until her reports stopped. Heglan Shadeeye lies dead in the wreckage of her hut north of the keep, and whatever killed her was not human."},{after=829,articles={"Dark Storms (quest)","Margoz (Classic)","Skull Rock (Classic)","Neeru Fireblade (quest)"},text="The Burning Blade has spread beyond the Valley of Trials. The goblin warlock Fizzle Darkstorm led its cultists in Thunder Ridge, and a larger band holds Skull Rock, a cave just outside Orgrimmar, where they burn their own flesh with searing collars to attune themselves to demonic power. The shaman Margoz couldn't read the collars' secrets, so one has gone to Neeru Fireblade, a warlock of Orgrimmar's Cleft of Shadow who claims he uses his arts only against demons. True or not, the Horde may need his help."},{after=924,articles={"Ak'Zeloth (Classic)","The Demon Seed (quest)"},text="The Burning Blade rallies around the Demon Seed, a tool of the Burning Legion kept in a cave atop Dreadmist Peak in the Barrens. Neeru wanted it destroyed, which his assistant Ak'Zeloth thought strange, and with one of Ak'Zeloth's power stones it is gone. But its destruction sent waves of staggering power through the ether, and Ak'Zeloth is sure that others felt them, and will wonder who broke their toy."}}},
[1412]={articles={"Mulgore","Mulgore (Classic)","Earthen Echo"},text="The green plains of Mulgore, home of the tauren, lie in the foothills of the Stonetalon Mountains, walled in by mountains on every side. Thunder Bluff towers over the northern plains, Bloodhoof Village stands at their heart, and young tauren train at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. On the Skywatcher Plateau in the northwest, Muln Earthfury and the elders of the Earthen Ring council study the relics brought to them from Kalimdor's buried ruins.\n\nFor all its peace, Mulgore has troubles: quilboar overrun Brambleblade Ravine and the sacred Red Rocks, the goblins of the Venture Company mine the eastern mountains, dwarves dig at the Bael'dun Digsite, Windfury harpies claim the far north, and Palemane gnolls camp in the south.",chapters={{after=757,articles={"Rites of the Earthmother","Rite of Strength (Classic)"},text="A young tauren earns the respect of Thunder Bluff's elders through the three Rites of the Earthmother: Strength, Vision and Wisdom. The first is a test of bravery against the tribe's enemies, the Bristleback quilboar of Brambleblade Ravine, who ambush hunting parties and steal from Camp Narache by night. With their belts brought to Chief Hawkwind, the first rite is passed."},{after=3376,articles={"Break Sharptusk!"},text="The tauren carved their home from this land at a cost, and the Bristlebacks' war on them has been long. Their chief, Sharptusk Thornmantle, led it from a makeshift village in Brambleblade Ravine. His head has gone to Brave Windfeather, a warning to all who would threaten the tauren's home."},{after=746,articles={"Mulgore (Classic)","Dwarven Digging"},text="Dwarves of the Alliance have opened the Bael'dun Digsite in the mountains of Mulgore, searching for traces of their own ancestry. Baine Bloodhoof agrees that secrets lie in the earth, but hollowing and defiling the land is no way to earn its teachings. Their tools are smashed now; the beasts of Mulgore may calm, and, spirits willing, the dwarves may learn."},{after=760,articles={"Poison Water (Classic)","Wildmane Cleansing (Classic)"},text="Goblins and their servants poisoned Mulgore's three sacred wells, Winterhoof, Thunderhorn and Wildmane. One by one they have been cleansed with totems made from the gifts of the land itself, and beasts drink from them eagerly again. Bloodhoof Village is grateful, and hopes the waters of Mulgore stay clean forever."},{after=773,articles={"Rite of Vision (2)","Rite of Wisdom (Classic)"},text="The Rite of Vision is drunk, not fought: the Water of the Seers, brewed from well stones and ambercorn, brings a vision before the tribal fire that leads the seeker onward. The Ancestral Spirits of Red Rocks, the tauren who gave their lives to found and defend Thunder Bluff, bless only those who have drunk it, and with the Rite of Wisdom they pass the duty of protecting the city to the young. With all three rites passed, a young tauren may enter Thunder Bluff with pride."},{after=770,articles={"The Demon Scarred Cloak"},text="The great wolf Ghost Howl roamed Mulgore with a terrible wound in its hide, gained in its battles against the Burning Legion. It has been slain at last, to the astonishment of the old hunter Skorn Whitecloud, who hopes its spirit has finally found peace."},{after=765,articles={"The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (1)","The Venture Co. (Classic)","Supervisor Fizsprocket (Classic)"},text="Tauren braves burned a Venture Company caravan on the shore of Stonebull Lake. Its crates held ore from a goblin mine in the eastern foothills, and papers laying out the company's plans to drive the tauren from their lands and plunder them freely. The mine has been sent a message the goblins can't ignore, and its supervisor, Fizsprocket, is dead; his papers may yet tell what the Venture Company means to do next."}}},
[1413]={articles={"The Barrens"},text="The Barrens is a vast, dry savanna in the heart of Kalimdor, held mostly by the Horde, with low mountains for its borders. Around the Crossroads in the north lie a handful of oases, fed by the waters of the Wailing Caverns, which carry a life energy that invigorates every plant and beast that drinks them.\n\nLong ago the Barrens was forest, tended by the night elves. The Burning Legion, war and the Great Sundering turned it into the barren expanse it is now, though there is more to it than dry soil and sand.",chapters={{after=880,articles={"The Barrens Oases","Altered Beings (Classic)"},text="The druids of Thunder Bluff sensed a strange power seeping into the Barrens, and sent Tonga Runetotem to the Crossroads to find its source. The new oases that have formed in recent years each hide a fissure, and the fissures seem to be what gives them their life. The snapjaws that drink from them have been changed by it: their shells told Tonga much, but raised more questions than they answered."},{after=850,articles={"Kolkar Leaders (Classic)"},text="The Kolkar centaurs have plagued the tauren for years, but they were never more than disorganised raiders here, until new leaders rose to rally them. Barak Kodobane, who camped west of the Forgotten Pools, was only one strand of a tangled thread: other Kolkar leaders still gather their warbands across the Barrens."},{after=4021,articles={"Counterattack! (Classic)"},text="The deaths of their leaders maddened the Kolkar rather than breaking them. A host came from their homeland in Desolace to take revenge, and threw itself against the Horde's bunkers west of the Crossroads. Regthar Deathgate's line held, their warlord Krom'zar fell, and word of the victory has gone to Thrall himself."},{after=876,articles={"Harpy Lieutenants (Classic)","Serena Bloodfeather (Classic)"},text="The Witchwing harpies of the Dry Hills have been raiding the Horde's caravans, and not for plunder alone. Their leader, Serena Bloodfeather, was avenging her sister Bloodfeather, slain by Rexxar years ago. Serena is dead now too, and Darsok Swiftdagger of the Crossroads meant to send her head out on the next caravan, as a warning to the rest of her kind."},{after=898,articles={"Free From the Hold"},text="On the coast, Theramore's soldiers hold Northwatch Hold, and they treat everyone they meet as an enemy of the Alliance. They sank a moonshine transport sailing between Ratchet and Booty Bay, and threw its only survivor, a deckhand named Gilthares Firebough, into a cell. He broke out at last and made it home to Ratchet, where his friends had long given him up for dead."},{after=95508,articles={"Trouble in the Valley","Unwelcome Guests (Barrens)"},text="The hermit tanner on the ridge above the Dry Hills is Walton, once a sailor of the Kul Tiras navy. When Lord Admiral Proudmoore took Theramore and began his campaign in Durotar, Walton objected to its methods, above all the execution of civilians, and deserted. Kul Tiran marines have come to the Barrens hunting for him, and with a little help, he lived through their visit; but his past has caught up with him at last."},{after=906,articles={"Tribes at War","Betrayal from Within (1)","Betrayal from Within (2)"},text="Mangletooth, a Razormane quilboar caged at Camp Taurajo, aided his tribe even from behind bars, and they left him there to rot. So he turned on them. Blessing the Horde with the power of Agamaggan, he had them weaken the rival Bristlebacks, then hunt down the three who came out of Razorfen Kraul to lead the raids on the Crossroads: Nak, Kuz and their leader, Lok Orcbane. Thork, who had blamed another quilboar for the raids, was astonished to be handed Lok's skull."},{after=849,articles={"Gann's Reclamation","Revenge of Gann (2)"},text="The dwarves of Bael Modan have blasted and torn at the land where Gann Stonespire's tribe once lived, killing many tauren and driving the rest from their birthplace, and every attempt at talks failed. Gann, who now wanders those roads alone, struck back: Prospector Khazgorm was killed, and the dig's flying machine was blown apart with explosives made from the dwarves' own supplies. The keep at Bael Modan still stands, built to guard the dig and now ready for war."},{after=97250,articles={"Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected"},text="Tension is rising along the eastern coast. Alliance soldiers have crossed into the Barrens at the border with Dustwallow Marsh, toppling the landmarks the tauren pathfinders left behind and blaming the Horde for a tavern burned to the ground. Camp Taurajo drove them back, though even its grunts doubt the Horde had a hand in the fire: too many of its own people rely on that road."}}},
[1420]={articles={"Tirisfal Glades","Bandarion Keep"},text="Tirisfal Glades, \"Tyr's Fall\", is an eerie, wooded region on the northern coast of Lordaeron, named for the Titan Keeper Tyr. It is the land of the Forsaken, soaked through with the Lich King's plague: its sky is a gloomy, blighted green, and its trees cling to their last shreds of life.\n\nThe Forsaken share it with old enemies: the Scarlet Crusade, whose Scarlet Monastery stands in the glades, and the Scourge, who haunt its derelict farms and mills. In the west, on the bluffs by the Whispering Forest, a new order of Forsaken paladins holds Bandarion Keep.",chapters={{after=364,articles={"Rude Awakening","The Mindless Ones"},text="The Forsaken rose in the Lich King's plague, but their new leader, Lady Sylvanas, the Dark Lady, freed them from his control. Now they wage war on his Scourge, and on the holdouts of humanity who dog their every step. The Mindless Ones who overran the north of Deathknell were once their brothers and sisters, but they are the Lich King's slaves still and will never join the Forsaken, so they must be destroyed."},{after=6395,articles={"Marla's Last Wish"},text="Before the plague, Marla Fipps lived with her husband Samuel. When it came, Samuel rose with the Scourge, and Marla, spared undeath, died at the hands of her mindless husband. Her dying wish was to be buried with him, and now Samuel lies beside her in the first row of Deathknell's graveyard, where perhaps they have found peace together."},{after=383,articles={"The Scarlet Crusade (quest)","The Red Messenger","Vital Intelligence (Classic)"},text="The Scarlet Crusade hunts every undead, Forsaken or Scourge, and won't rest until all are destroyed. A messenger it sent from the Scarlet Monastery to Deathknell carried a map of its field commanders and agents in Tirisfal, and that map is now with Executor Zygand in Brill. With it, the Forsaken could kill the Crusade's leaders in the glades in one swift stroke."},{after=492,articles={"A New Plague","A New Plague (4)"},text="Lady Sylvanas has challenged the Royal Apothecary Society to brew a new plague, deadlier than any ailment on Azeroth, to bring Arthas's Scourge to ruin. Apothecary Johaan of Brill boiled darkhound blood, murloc scales and spider venom into his first concoction, and tested it on a captured dwarven mountaineer held in the cellar of the Gallows' End Tavern. The drink, Johaan says, holds a subtle hint of what the Dark Lady has planned for the rest of Azeroth."},{after=411,articles={"The Lich's Identity","Proving Allegiance","The Prodigal Lich Returns"},text="A free-willed lich on the island in Brightwater Lake set his minions on anyone who came near, believing every other undead a slave of the Scourge. His spellbook named him: Gunther Arcanus, a skilled necromancer in life and, before the plague, a comrade-in-arms of the Undercity mage Bethor Iceshard. The death of the Scourge agent Lillith Nefara proved to him that others had broken free of the Lich King, and through a nether gem he now speaks with his old friend: the Forsaken have won him over."},{after=372,articles={"At War With The Scarlet Crusade (Classic)","At War With The Scarlet Crusade (2)","At War With The Scarlet Crusade (3)","At War With The Scarlet Crusade (4)"},text="With the documents from Deathknell, the Deathguard knew where every Scarlet camp in Tirisfal lay, and one by one their commanders have fallen: Captain Perrine in the ruined tower southwest of Brill, Captain Vachon near the Balnir Farmstead, and the ruthless Captain Melrache at the watch post past Faol's Rest. Executor Zygand counts each death a step closer to defeating Arthas once and for all."},{after=96898,articles={"Remnants of War"},text="Not long ago, the necropolis Naxxramas bore down on the Undercity, meaning to wipe the Forsaken off the map. The Forsaken and the Argent Dawn drove it off, and both paid dearly for it. In the battle, the great necrotic crystal atop Naxxramas shattered, scattering its fragments across the glades, and the Cult of the Damned has come to gather them. Even spent, the shards are dangerous to touch."},{after=96899,articles={"Bandarion Keep (quest)","Bandarion Keep"},text="Bandarion Keep was once Dawn's Watch, where the Argent Dawn readied itself against the Scourge's last push into Tirisfal. When the Dawn withdrew to the Plaguelands it fell into disrepair, until Danitha Morr and her paladins took it over, restored it, and named it after a friend she shared with the Argent emissary Leonid Barthalomew."},{after=96896,articles={"A Righteous Cause","Bandarion Keep"},text="That friend was Garek Bandarion, perhaps the first Forsaken to wield the Light again, who fell defending the Undercity against Naxxramas. Leonid came on behalf of Lord Tyrosus to offer Danitha's order a place in the Argent Dawn, where its Light would be valued. She refused: the Forsaken are still the people her paladins swore to protect. Many Forsaken, remembering what the living did in the Light's name, mistrust paladins deeply, and that is why she means to stay."},{after=99153,articles={"As Above, So Below","The One That Got Away (Forever)"},text="Beneath Shadowvale, the ruined village southwest of Bandarion Keep, the cellars had been festering into a Scourge uprising that went unnoticed for far too long. One of the cultists gathering the Naxxramas fragments escaped there with a piece of the crystal that still had power in it, enough to raise the dead or make them stronger. The crystal has been found before things got out of hand, and the glowing bones taken from the cellars may show the paladins how the threat grew in secret."},{after=91317,articles={"The Tarnished"},text="Not every risen paladin has made peace with undeath. The Tarnished carried their old prejudices into it, and from the ruins of Shadowvale they turned their blades on their fellow Forsaken, each killing feeding the mistrust Danitha's order is trying to overcome. With their commander dead, the order's place in Tirisfal is a little surer, though Danitha expects it may take years, even decades, for the Forsaken to embrace them."},{after=408,articles={"Deaths in the Family (Classic)","Speak with Sevren (Classic)","The Family Crypt (Classic)"},text="The Agamands were once the most prosperous family in Tirisfal. When the Scourge came, they fortified their mills and persuaded their workers to stay and defend them, dooming them all to undeath. The mills became a Scourge foothold, and in the family crypt the skeletal Captain Dargol raised the Agamand ancestors to turn against the Forsaken. His skull is now in Magistrate Sevren's hands, a telling blow the Scourge won't fail to notice."},{after=99156,articles={"Rear Guard Patrol","Rear Guard Patrol (Forever)"},text="The Bulwark holds the line between Tirisfal and the Plaguelands, and it can only hold if no Scourge strike from behind or cut its supplies; the Balnir Farmstead had become a haven for those that slipped past. Then a rogue abomination, Riptear, was seen near Venomweb Vale, and abominations don't simply slip through the Bulwark unnoticed. Riptear is dead, but the Deathguard are keeping the matter quiet: something is wrong."}}},
[1426]={articles={"Dun Morogh"},text="Dun Morogh is a range of snowy, forested mountains in western Khaz Modan, the homeland of the dwarves and gnomes. Ironforge is carved into Ironforge Mountain, and Gnomeregan lies in the foothills of Chill Breeze Valley.\n\nIts peaks are the highest in the Eastern Kingdoms: their glaciers feed the rivers of Khaz Modan, and their rock gives the dwarves all the ore they could want. Mountains wall it in on every side, and dwarven troops watch the few passes in.",chapters={{after=170,articles={"A New Threat (Dun Morogh)"},text="Troggs have been bursting out of the ground all across the dwarves' lands, and Coldridge Valley is no exception: a few nights ago they overran the camp to the west. Balir Frosthammer's shorthanded people have pushed them back from the hills and the frozen lake for now. If Coldridge is any sign of what is happening elsewhere, Balir fears hard times ahead, and hopes the king and the Senate are acting."},{after=3361,articles={"A Refugee's Quandary (Classic)"},text="The gnomes drove the troggs out of Gnomeregan, but then something went horribly wrong, and their home is now irradiated. Its people fled in every direction and are scattered across Dun Morogh. Felix Whindlebolt reached Anvilmar with nothing, his belongings carried off to the Frostmane trolls' camps, until a stranger brought them back."},{after=218,articles={"The Stolen Journal","The Troll Cave"},text="With the Senate's hands full of troggs, it sent the brothers Grelin and Senir Whitebeard to judge the threat from the Frostmane trolls. In Coldridge Valley the trolls hold little more than the southern cave, and though a band of them stole Grelin's journal in the night, a few strong arms were enough to deal with them. With the journal back, Grelin judges the trolls here no great cause for worry, and readies his report for his brother."},{after=99161,articles={"Rime's Wrath (2)","Rime's Wrath","Finding Warmth"},text="Father Gavin, a priest living as a hermit in the mountains east of Kharanos, keeps his hearth open to any traveller. A few days ago a shelf of rock and ice broke from the peaks, and the slide stirred up the ice elementals, making roads that are dangerous enough already worse still. The first and largest of them, which Gavin called Avala, had been shifting the weather and setting off smaller slides; it is destroyed now. Gavin, no fighter any more, wishes the spirits could have been calmed instead, but hopes fewer lives will be lost on the road."},{after=412,articles={"Operation Recombobulation (Classic)"},text="Gnomeregan's disaster was the gnomes' own doing: their attempt to wipe out the troggs failed, and instead turned most of their people into mindless, vicious leper gnomes. In Kharanos, Razzle Sprysprocket and Ozzie are building a machine to reverse the change, the Recombobulator, and the cogs and gears taken from the lepers outside Gnomeregan have nearly finished it. Razzle is sure it will make the gnomish race as good as new."},{after=433,articles={"The Public Servant (Classic)"},text="When the dwarves dug deep into Gol'Bolar Quarry, troggs poured out of the earth, wrecking the equipment and driving the miners out. Senator Mehr Stonehallow, of the Senate and the Explorers' League, has taken this part of the infestation as his own charge. The troggs have been thinned: a start, he says, enough perhaps for mountaineers to move in and secure the quarry so the miners can go back to work."},{after=417,articles={"A Pilot's Revenge (Classic)","The Lost Pilot (Classic)"},text="Mori Hildelve, a siege engine pilot, broke his engine on a steep hill while searching for a rare ore needed for high-grade blastpowder, and went on alone on foot. His frozen, mauled body was found in the hills with his journal: the great ice claw bear Mangeclaw had savaged him, and later came back to finish him off. His last wish was revenge, and Mangeclaw is dead; the pilots of the Ironforge Siege Brigade will remember the deed."},{after=96391,articles={"Underground Map","Nip 'Em in the Bud","Farsen's Watch"},text="Earthseer Farsen, a visitor from Aerie Peak camped above Gol'Bolar Quarry, used his farsight to show how thick the Dark Iron dwarves have grown in Dun Morogh, and sent adventurers against their spies at Ironband's Compound. One carried a map of tunnels and underground highways, written in the Dark Irons' jagged runes, all meeting at a point beneath Ironforge itself. Farsen reads it as the plan for an incursion into the vaults under the city, and the spies as its scouts; if the dwarves hurry, they might beat the Dark Irons to their goal."}}},
[1429]={articles={"Elwynn Forest"},text="Elwynn Forest is a vast woodland of stately hardwoods, the heartland of the human kingdom of Stormwind and where every human adventurer begins. Stormwind City lies to the northwest, the Redridge Mountains to the east, and Duskwood to the south, across the river.\n\nMuch of the forest burned during the First War, but its trees and glades have grown back. Marshal McBride keeps the peace in Northshire Valley, and Marshal Dughan in Goldshire.",chapters={{after=21,articles={"Skirmish at Echo Ridge","Investigate Echo Ridge","Kobold Camp Cleanup"},text="Kobolds have crept into Northshire from the woods to the north, and a band of their workers has dug into the Echo Ridge Mine. One by one they are thieves and cowards, but in numbers they are a real threat, and Marshal McBride wanted them purged before they dug in deeper. The mine is clearer now, though McBride admits there may still be kobolds in its tunnels."},{after=6,articles={"Bounty on Garrick Padfoot","Brotherhood of Thieves"},text="A new gang of thieves has appeared across the river east of the abbey, calling themselves the Defias Brotherhood and wearing bandanas. One of them, the cutthroat Garrick Padfoot, gathered a band of thugs at a shack by the vineyards and preyed on Northshire's farmers and merchants for weeks. Garrick is dead and his bounty paid, but what the Brotherhood itself is after, no one in the valley can say."},{after=87,articles={"Goldtooth (quest)","Lost Necklace","Pie for Billy","Back to Billy"},text="Old Bernice Stonefield, a widow, lost her necklace and blamed young Billy Maclure from the neighbouring farm. Bribed with a pie baked from boar meat, Billy let slip that he had dropped it near the Fargodeep Mine, where a big, gold-toothed kobold snatched it up and carried it off to his den. The necklace is home again, and Bernice gave away her late husband's lucky keepsake, which he had forgotten on his last campaign."},{after=114,articles={"The Escape (Alliance)","Young Lovers","Speak with Gramma","Note to William","Collecting Kelp"},text="Maybell Maclure and Tommy Joe Stonefield are in love, but their families are bitter enemies and keep them apart. Tommy Joe's grandmother saw no future for the pair while the feud lasts, but wanted to steal them a little time together, so William Pestle at the Lion's Pride Inn brewed an invisibility liquor from crystal kelp taken from the murlocs of Crystal Lake. Maybell feels guilty deceiving her family, but she means to drink it at the first chance and slip away to Tommy Joe."},{after=39,articles={"Deliver Thomas' Report","Report to Thomas","Discover Rolf's Fate","Find the Lost Guards","Further Concerns","A Fishy Peril"},text="Murlocs have swum up the streams of eastern Elwynn, but Marshal Dughan, busy with gnolls and bandits, wanted a soldier's report before he would believe they were a danger. Guard Thomas, posted at the eastern bridge, had sent two footmen up the river, and both are dead: Malakai Stone on the riverbank, and Rolf Hartford in a murloc village on the shore of Stone Cairn Lake. Dughan is convinced now, but his defences were stretched thin before he lost them, and he fears there will be fighting in Goldshire within the week."},{after=147,articles={"Manhunt (Classic)","The Collector (Classic)"},text="A pickup schedule taken from the Defias showed that someone known only as the Collector was receiving shipments of gold from Elwynn's mines, from a hideout at the Brackwell Pumpkin Patch. The Collector is dead, and on him was a membership ring of Stormwind's old Stonemason's Guild. Why a common thief would wear an artisan guild's ring, and why the Defias are stealing gold from the kingdom's mines, Marshal Dughan cannot yet say."},{after=176,articles={"Wanted: \"Hogger\"","Westbrook Garrison Needs Help!"},text="Gnolls and thieves are pressing on Elwynn's western border, and Westbrook Garrison has asked Goldshire for soldiers the army cannot spare. The worst of the gnolls was Hogger, a huge brute who prowled the southwestern woods and overpowered every attempt to capture him, until the Stormwind Army put a bounty on his head. Hogger is dead, and Marshal Dughan, who had begun to think no one could do it, paid out the reward with relief."}}},
[1438]={articles={"Teldrassil"},text="Teldrassil, \"Crown of the Earth\", is the World Tree the night elves grew on an island off Kalimdor's northwestern coast after the Third War. Its crown holds their capital, Darnassus, and its boughs hold Rut'theran Village, Dolanaar and Shadowglen, amid twilit forests with a world of their own.\n\nIt is the Alliance's greatest stronghold in Kalimdor, and young night elves set out into the world from its shelter.",chapters={{after=97977,articles={"Nature's Call (Forever)","The Woodland Protector (Classic)"},text="The dryad Tarindrella, a protector of the forests who had not been felt in Kalimdor for years, has returned to Shadowglen, and that alone means something is wrong. The Gnarlpine furbolgs, the night elves' old allies, have abandoned their camps in the glen, and the totems they left behind tell why: they began to dream of being wiped out by a great shadow, until the fear became rage and the shadow took the shape of their elven friends. Tarindrella hopes their plight will help explain what is happening on Teldrassil."},{after=2561,articles={"Druid of the Claw (quest)","The Sleeping Druid"},text="In the Ban'ethil Barrow Den, Gnarlpine shaman found a way to tear a sleeping druid's spirit from his body. Oben Rageclaw was trapped in the Emerald Dream while the furbolgs sent his empty body against anyone exploring the den. The only way to free him was to kill that body and use the shamans' charm on it; now his spirit is free of them, to rest in the Dream for good."},{after=486,articles={"Ursal the Mauler (quest)","The Relics of Wakening","Gnarlpine Corruption (Classic)","A Troubling Breeze"},text="In the Ban'ethil Barrow Den the Druids of the Talon sleep to keep their pact with Ysera, guarded by the Great Warden Gaerolas Talvethren. The Gnarlpine, driven mad by their chieftain Ursal the Mauler and the fel moss he wields, ambushed the warden, pillaged Starbreeze Village and overran the den. Athridas Bearmantle used the Relics of Wakening to rouse the sleepers, a ritual that could only take hold once Ursal was dead. The druids are safe, but the source of Ursal's corruption is still somewhere in the forest."},{after=489,articles={"Seek Redemption!","Zenn's Bidding"},text="Zenn Foulhoof, who paid well for nightsaber fangs, owl feathers and spider silk, is a satyr, an enemy of the forest, and those who ran his errands killed Nature's creatures for him. To win back the trust of the Council of the Forest, they fed him fel cones, corrupted seeds that fall from the trees, billowing green smoke. Thinking them a harmless snack, he ate them, and was turned into a frog."},{after=2499,articles={"Oakenscowl (Classic)","Return to Denalan","Tumors","Rellian Greenspyre (Classic)"},text="A sickness is spreading among Teldrassil's timberlings: mossy tumours full of poison grow on them, and even their seeds carry the taint. Rellian Greenspyre in Darnassus could do nothing for them, and the worst afflicted, around Wellspring Lake, had to be cut down. Denalan, who studies the tree's plant life at Lake Al'Ameth, traced the corruption there to Oakenscowl, once a great leader of the timberlings, poisoned by a gargantuan tumour. Oakenscowl is dead and Denalan has the tumour to study, but the disease itself is still a mystery."},{after=99073,articles={"Easing Suffering","The Great Tree Provides","Not Dead Yet","The Lost Runner"},text="The Oracle Tree sends messages to the elders, and Sentinels posted at its glade carry them to Darnassus. The latest runner, Eralya Leafshadow, still in training, never arrived: she lay dying on the road, attacked by harpies sick with the same foulness spreading through the forest, and that rot had taken hold of her too. Byancie of Dolanaar brewed a salve from lasher fronds and spring water; it brought Eralya some peace, though Byancie could not promise it would save her."},{after=935,articles={"Crown of the Earth (6)","Crown of the Earth (5)","Crown of the Earth (4)","Crown of the Earth (3)","Crown of the Earth"},text="At Mount Hyjal, Nordrassil's power was spent to defeat Archimonde, and with it went the night elves' immortality; then Illidan the Betrayer went free, and Malfurion Stormrage vanished. Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm took the lead and, with the Circle of Ancients' approval, grew Teldrassil to win their immortality back. But Nozdormu refused to bless the new World Tree, chiding Staghelm for his arrogance, and Alexstrasza refused with him. Without their blessings Teldrassil's growth is flawed and unpredictable, which is why strange beasts rise from its soil and its furbolgs have gone mad."},{after=940,articles={"Teldrassil (quest)","The Enchanted Glade (Classic)"},text="Sentinel Arynia Cloudsbreak and a few Sentinels guard the Oracle Tree from the harpies nesting all around its glade, and the last runner the tree sent to Darnassus was killed by them. With the harpies driven from their nests, the tree trusted its message for Fandral Staghelm to the one who drove them off. Some of Teldrassil's troubles have eased, he found, while others have grown, and he fears his work on the tree, and the night elves' immortality, may never be restored."}}},
[1442]={articles={"Stonetalon Mountains"},text="The Stonetalon Mountains rise west of the Barrens, between Ashenvale and Desolace. They were the harpies' ancestral home, though turmoil has thinned their numbers, and Stonetalon Peak, the tallest of them, has long been sacred to both the night elves and the tauren.\n\nThe tauren hold Sun Rock Retreat at the heart of the range, and the Darkspear trolls a village at Malaka'jin in the south. Windshear Crag has been ravaged by years of logging, its air thick with soot and its water fouled with oil, and beyond it the Charred Vale runs down to the sea, home to black dragons and elementals.",chapters={{after=6629,articles={"Avenge My Village","Kill Grundig Darkcloud"},text="The Grimtotem clan raided Makaba Flathoof's village on the southeastern edge of Stonetalon and killed nearly everyone there; Makaba fought until he could fight no more, and barely escaped with his life. Grundig Darkcloud, who led the attack, has since been slain at Grimtotem Post along with his brutes, but Makaba will never forget what the Grimtotem did."},{after=6401,articles={"Protect Kaya","Kaya's Alive"},text="Makaba Flathoof's daughter Kaya, believed lost in the attack on their village, was alive after all: a captive, until a stranger set her free and saw her safely to Camp Aparaje. Her aunt, Tammra Windfield of Sun Rock Retreat, has heard the good news."},{after=1062,articles={"Goblin Invaders"},text="The goblin-run Venture Company has moved into Stonetalon, felling its trees and burning great stretches of forest, and the spirits of the mountains are nearly blind with pain and rage. The Horde has struck back at the loggers in Windshear Crag, but it may take the wisdom of the tauren elders to calm the spirits."},{after=6481,articles={"Boulderslide Ravine (quest)","Earthen Arise"},text="The kobolds of Boulderslide Ravine were frantically mining resonite, a rare crystal that Mor'rogal of Sun Rock Retreat found to carry traces of earthen magic. Deep in their cave they had uncovered Goggeroc, an earthen, one of the beings the Titans made to shape the land, asleep in a resonite cask. Mor'rogal enchanted the crystals to break the cask open while the earthen was still weak from his long slumber, and Goggeroc was slain before he could threaten Stonetalon."},{after=1096,articles={"Super Reaper 6000 (quest)","Further Instructions","Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle (quest)"},text="The Venture Company's works in Stonetalon are run by a gnome, Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle, his rotting flesh grafted with metal and gears, who watches over them from high above Cragpool Lake. Ziz Fizziks, a goblin he threw out, now works for Sputtervalve of Ratchet, who wanted the plans for the company's enormous woodcutting machine, the Super Reaper 6000. The plans are in Sputtervalve's hands now, and Gerenzo is dead."},{after=6301,articles={"Cycle of Rebirth"},text="At Sun Rock Retreat, Tammra Windfield is working to heal what industrial greed and endless war have done to Stonetalon. She gathers Gaea seeds from the shores of Mirkfallon Lake and imbues them with the Earthmother's blessing, so that new life can take root even in the Charred Vale."},{after=6283,articles={"Harpies Threaten","Bloodfury Bloodline"},text="The Bloodfury harpies of the Charred Vale are the source of Kalimdor's growing numbers of harpies, preying on every living thing within their reach. Maggran Earthbinder of Sun Rock Retreat, for whom the tauren are the protectors of these lands, called for them to be culled, and their leader, Bloodfury Ripper, all but a queen to them, has been slain in the Vale's western hills. Without her, Maggran hopes, their numbers will dwindle."},{after=1088,articles={"Cenarius' Legacy","Ordanus (quest)"},text="Not everyone at Sun Rock Retreat loves the land. Braelyn Firehand, who longs to return to Jaedenar but whose master will not allow it until her work is done, hates the druids of Stonetalon Peak for stifling the flow of magic, and has turned Horde adventurers against the Sons and Daughters of Cenarius there. The druids spirited Keeper Ordanus, one of the forest god's eldest sons, away to Raynewood Retreat in Ashenvale, but Braelyn's magic found him, and his head is hers now. She knew him once, long ago, she says: he held himself above even the Highborne."}}},
[2521]={articles={"Zephras Isle","Skyborne"},text="Zephras Isle is a flying island in Skywall, the Elemental Plane of Air, and the home of the Skyborne. The air elementals who once lived there beside them vanished years ago, and since then the magic holding the island up has been fading.\n\nThe Skyborne's other islands, Anvilas and Siroccas, have fallen silent and are collapsing, and their refugees are fleeing to Zephras. But Zephras is starting to fall apart too.\n\nThe Skyborne, who call themselves the shen'dorei, descend from Highborne who left Eldre'Thalas some ten thousand years ago, and lived beside the wind spirits ever since. An elected Council of Elders governs them, led on Zephras by High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong. Two orders guide their people: the Windshapers, shaman who honour the elements, and the High Order, heirs of the Highborne magisters, who seek the arcane lore of their forebears.",chapters={{after=92465,articles={"Agitators","Return to Rorian"},text="Not every shen'dorei is willing to wait for the spirits to return. The Al'Aketh cult has been stirring the simple wind sprites by Thendal's standing stones into violence, and shen'dorei blood has now been shed in the grove. Rorian the Dayseeker had long watched the cult from afar; this, he says, is a clear escalation."},{after=92472,articles={"The Next Step (Skyborne)","Skyborne"},text="The Al'Aketh are zealots devoted to Al'Akir the Windlord, the elemental ruler of Skywall, a name the wind spirits rarely spoke. Their leaders are what remains of an older order of his priests, grown strong on the fear the spirits' disappearance left behind, and some of the refugees from the failing islands have joined them just to survive. Brutal and single-minded, they now threaten Shen'dar Village to the south."},{after=94411,articles={"Skyborne"},text="In the Shen'dar Highlands, the old quarrel between the two orders has turned ugly. Apprentices of the High Order keep meddling at the Windshapers' sacred standing stones, whatever they are told, and Elder Illaya Amberwind, loath as she is to see shen'dorei blood spilled, has had them driven off by force. To the Windshapers, the magic that binds the island is fragile, and one careless spell could bring it down."},{after=94413,articles={"Skyborne"},text="In the Shen'dar Highlands, the old quarrel between the two orders has turned ugly. As Zephras cracks, ley lines have begun breaking through the ground: raw arcane power to the High Order, scars to be healed to the Windshapers. When Windshaper novices set out to close them, Elder Rathiril Sunlance of the High Order had them killed."},{after=93927,articles={"Skyborne"},text="Shen'dar Village tolerated an Al'Aketh missionary, Sania Silverstream, to keep the peace, but that peace was always an illusion. Posing as a new recruit in the cult's stronghold of Falaath Village, the hero learned that High Priestess Lorthuna, the leader of the whole cult, had come in person to plan attacks on Shen'dar and its western watchtower. By the time help reached the tower, its peacekeepers lay dead, burned by storm-wielding priests who had brought a great cache of windstones to do something to the anchor pylon there. Why the cult wants the pylon, no one yet knows."},{after=93746,articles={"A Firm Response","Skyborne"},text="The crisis has strained the bond between the two orders. One of the few working constructs the wind spirits left in Valanaar was found stripped of its parts by Belathaan Brightwish, a High Order magister whose own mother was a Windshaper, and Grand Skyseer Ayessa Dawnsinger sent someone to frighten him off. On the road to the Shrine of Akir, Belathaan turned out to be meeting High Priestess Lorthuna, sent by the High Order's Supreme Magister, Elaadrin Evengale, to talk peace. Lorthuna had him killed instead, and his death is one more grievance between the orders."},{after=92703,articles={"Skyborne"},text="In the lowlands west of Valanaar, a gang of bandits led by Ferauu has been preying on Windfield Orchard. Alvarion Windfield, badly wounded, went to the city for fighters, while his wife Aamelia stayed to make her stand. With a stranger's help she faced Ferauu's thugs and won, aided by the old cyclone construct that has wandered her fields for decades: a relic of the wind spirits whose purpose no one remembers."},{after=94568,articles={"Skyborne","High Priestess Lorthuna (Forever)"},text="The cult struck the eastern watchtower too, led by Belguilos the Breaker, once a peacekeeper herself, and only just failed. A turncoat who offered to reveal the cult's plans was murdered before he could be brought in, but he left behind a damaged recall crystal: Lorthuna had ordered her forces to march. The anchor pylons do more than hold Zephras up; they also hide it from the eyes of Skywall. High Elder Shadowsong concluded that Lorthuna means to bring down the wards and let the Windlord in."},{after=94491,articles={"Skyborne"},text="Deep in Shadowgale Forest, not far from the ruins of fallen Ban'aethal, the secretive Nightclaw druids keep a conclave in a cave. The Al'Aketh fell on it without warning, led by Haalien, a druid of the circle and a cousin of Elegael Thornpaw, and many of the druids died. A missive found on Haalien's body showed that the cult had come for a pylon nearby, and that its orders came from Valanaar: someone in the city is feeding the cult. Lotheluum Starbreeze has taken the evidence to the High Elder."},{after=92646,articles={"High Priestess Lorthuna (Forever)","Skyborne"},text="The Peacekeepers, Windshapers and High Order stormed the Shrine of Akir together, under a truce both orders swore would be only temporary, and many died on both sides. Above the shrine, Ayessa and Elaadrin found Lorthuna, Ayessa's own sister, lighting a beacon: the wards had been down for days. Rohash of Al'Akir's Conclave of Wind answered it, promised that the Windlord's judgement would come, and knocked Lorthuna away into the endless sky for her presumption. When his elementals attacked, the Earthen Ring and the Kirin Tor came through a portal to help, and Muln Earthfury persuaded Rohash to withdraw; but Rohash left vowing to tell Al'Akir everything."},{after=93835,articles={"High Priestess Lorthuna (Forever)","Skyborne"},text="The Peacekeepers, High Order and Windshapers stormed the Shrine of Akir together, under a truce both orders swore would be only temporary, and many died on both sides. Above the shrine, Elaadrin and Ayessa found Lorthuna, Ayessa's own sister, lighting a beacon: the wards had been down for days. Rohash of Al'Akir's Conclave of Wind answered it, promised that the Windlord's judgement would come, and knocked Lorthuna away into the endless sky for her presumption. When his elementals attacked, the Kirin Tor and the Earthen Ring came through a portal Archmage Ansirem Runeweaver had opened, and Muln Earthfury persuaded Rohash to withdraw; but Rohash left vowing to tell Al'Akir everything."},{after=93090,articles={"What Comes Next (Horde)"},text="With the Al'Aketh defeated, the Windshapers have found new friends in the Earthen Ring and, through them, joined the Horde. With High Elder Shadowsong's blessing, their shaman are leaving Zephras to seek a lasting answer to the island's failing magic. Muln Earthfury has helped open a skycutter route to Mulgore, where the Earthen Ring make their home, and some of the Windshapers are already waiting on the other side."},{after=93089,articles={"What Comes Next (Alliance)","Skyborne"},text="With the Al'Aketh defeated, the High Order has found new friends in the Kirin Tor of Dalaran and, through them, joined the Alliance. With High Elder Shadowsong's blessing, its magisters are leaving Zephras to recover the lost arcane knowledge of their Highborne forebears on Azeroth, and with it an answer to the island's failing magic. Archmage Ansirem Runeweaver has helped open a skycutter route between Zephras and Dalaran, and after much testing, the High Order's vanguard is ready to depart."}}},
}
